    "passageId": "culpeper-1652:herbal:chervil:009",
    "text": "The Garden Chervil being eaten, doth moderately warm the Stomach, and is a certain remedy (saith Tragus) to dissolve congealed or clotted Bloud in the Body, or that which is clotted by bruises, fals, &c. The Juyce or distilled Water therof being drunk, and the bruised Leavs laid to the place, being taken either in meat or drink, it is held good to provoke Urin, to expel the Stone in the Kidnies, to send down Womens Courses, and to help the Plurisie and prickings of the Sides.",
